<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd Allah ibn Yahya al-Kindi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 748</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Talib al-Haqq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> ibn Yahya al-Kindi (died 748), known as Talib al-Haqq, 'seeker of truth', was a judge in Hadramawt who in 746 was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> by Ibadi missionaries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>. He seized Sanaa and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, and his forces under Abu Hamza took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 747. He was killed in 748 by the army of the last Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan II</a>.</p></div>
